85,1.229-1.125s.54-.922.69-1.447c.149-.525.249-1.075.3-1.65.05-.575.075-1.162.075-1.762s-.025-1.188-.075-1.763c-.051-.575-.15-1.125-.3-1.65-.15-.525-.381-1.005-.69-1.44s-.72-.808-1.229-1.118v-.675c.92.36,1.662.83,2.227,1.41.565.58.978,1.298,1.238,2.153.26.855.39,1.882.39,3.083s-.13,2.232-.39,3.097c-.261.865-.673,1.585-1.238,2.16-.564.575-1.307,1.047-2.227,1.417v-.69Z\" stroke-width=\"0\"></path><circle cx=\"95.832\" cy=\"106.04\" r=\"53.294\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-miterlimit=\"10\" stroke-width=\"2\"></circle><path d=\"m149.126,110.109c1.061,0,2.077-.423,2.828-1.172.749-.751,1.172-1.771,1.172-2.828,0-1.063-.423-2.079-1.172-2.828-.751-.751-1.767-1.172-2.828-1.172s-2.079.421-2.829,1.172c-.749.749-1.171,1.765-1.171,2.828,0,1.057.421,2.077,1.171,2.828.751.749,1.767,1.172,2.829,1.172h0Zm0,0\" stroke-width=\"0\"></path><path d=\"m109.476,58.461c1.061,0,2.077-.423,2.828-1.172.749-.751,1.172-1.771,1.172-2.828,0-1.063-.423-2.079-1.172-2.828-.751-.751-1.767-1.172-2.828-1.172s-2.079.421-2.829,1.172c-.749.749-1.171,1.765-1.171,2.828,0,1.057.421,2.077,1.171,2.828.751.749,1.767,1.172,2.829,1.172h0Zm0,0\" stroke-width=\"0\"></path></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for circle\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>The center of the circle is the origin.</li><li>The following points are on the circle:<ul><li>(2 comma k)</li><li>(8 comma 0)</li></ul></li><li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li></ul></div></figure></p><p></p><p>The circle shown has its center at $(0,0)$. What is the value of $k$?</p>","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$6$"},{"label":"B","text":"$7$"},{"label":"C","text":"$8$"},{"label":"D","text":"$\\sqrt{60}$"}],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Choice D is correct. It’s given that the circle shown has its center at $(0,0)$. The equation of a circle in the xy-plane with its center at $(0,0)$ can be expressed as $x^2+y^2=r^2$, where $r$ is the radius of the circle. It’s shown that $(8,0)$ lies on the circle. Since the circle has its center at $(0,0)$ and $(8,0)$ lies on the circle, it follows that the radius of the circle is $8$. Substituting $8$ for $r$ in the equation $x^2+y^2=r^2$ yields $x^2+y^2=64$. It's also shown that $(2,k)$ lies on the circle. Substituting $2$ for $x$ and $k$ for $y$ in this equation yields $2^2+k^2=64$, or $4+k^2=64$. Subtracting $4$ from both sides of this equation yields $k^2=60$. Taking the square root of both sides of this equation yields $k=\\pm\\sqrt{60}$. Since $(2,k)$ lies above the x-axis, $k$ must be positive. Therefore $k=\\sqrt{60}$.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice C is incorrect. This is the x-coordinate of one of the circle’s x-intercepts, not the value of $k$.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"1fe41c6b","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Area and volume","difficulty":"hard","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"The table gives the areas and perimeters of two similar rectangles, where $n$ is a constant.\nArea (square inches)\nPerimeter (inches)\nRectangle A\n$630$\n$210$\nRectangle B\n$2,520$\n$n$\nWhat is the value of $n$?","question_text_html":"<p>The table gives the areas and perimeters of two similar rectangles, where $n$ is a constant.</p><figure><table><thead><tr><td></td><th scope=\"col\">Area (square inches)</th><th scope=\"col\">Perimeter (inches)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rectangle A</th><td>$630$</td><td>$210$</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rectangle B</th><td>$2,520$</td><td>$n$</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p>What is the value of $n$?</p>","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$2,100$"},{"label":"B","text":"$1,680$"},{"label":"C","text":"$840$"},{"label":"D","text":"$420$"}],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Choice D is correct. It’s given that rectangle A and rectangle B are similar. Therefore, there is a positive constant $k$ such that the length of each side of rectangle B is $k$ times the length of the corresponding side of rectangle A and the area of rectangle B is $k^2$ times the area of rectangle A. It’s also given that the area of rectangle A is $630$ square inches and that the area of rectangle B is $2,520$ square inches. It follows that $2,520=630k^2$. Dividing both sides of this equation by $630$ yields $4=k^2$, or $k^2=4$. Taking the positive square root of each side of this equation yields $k=2$. Therefore, the length of each side of rectangle B is $2$ times the length of the corresponding side of rectangle A. It follows that the perimeter of rectangle B is $2$ times the perimeter of rectangle A. Since the perimeter of rectangle A is $210$ inches, the perimeter of rectangle B is $2(210)$, or $420$, inches. Therefore, $n=420$.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice C is incorrect and may result from using the area scale factor instead of the side length scale factor.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"1dc7e423","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"hard","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"In triangle $ABC$, the measure of angle $A$ is $52^\\circ$ and $AC=30$. In triangle $PQR$, the measure of angle $P$ is $52^\\circ$ and $PR=120$. Which additional piece of information is sufficient to prove that triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $PQR$?","question_text_html":null,"choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$AB=50$ and $PQ=50$."},{"label":"B","text":"$AB=50$ and $QR=200$."},{"label":"C","text":"The measures of angle $B$ and angle $R$ are $32^\\circ$ and $96^\\circ$, respectively."},{"label":"D","text":"The measures of angle $B$ and angle $Q$ are $52^\\circ$ and $32^\\circ$, respectively."}],"correct_answer":"C","explanation":"Choice C is correct. It’s given that the measure of angle $A$ is $52^\\circ$ and the measure of angle $P$ is $52^\\circ$. Therefore, angle $A$ is congruent to angle $P$. Choice C states that the measures of angle $B$ and angle $R$ are $32^\\circ$ and $96^\\circ$, respectively. The triangle sum theorem states that the measures of the angles in a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$. In triangle $ABC$, if the measure of angle $B$ is $32^\\circ$, it follows that the measure of angle $C$ is $(180-(52+32))^\\circ$, or $96^\\circ$. In triangle $PQR$, if the measure of angle $R$ is $96^\\circ$, it follows that the measure of angle $Q$ is $(180-(52+96))^\\circ$, or $32^\\circ$. Therefore, in triangles $ABC$ and $PQR$, angle $B$ is congruent to angle $Q$, and angle $C$ is congruent to angle $R$. If two triangles have three pairs of congruent angles, then the triangles are similar. Therefore, the additional piece of information that is sufficient to prove that triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $PQR$ is that the measures of angle $B$ and angle $R$ are $32^\\circ$ and $96^\\circ$, respectively.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice D is incorrect. If the measure of angle $B$ is $52^\\circ$, it follows that the measure of angle $C$ is $(180-(52+52))^\\circ$, or $76^\\circ$. If the measure of angle $Q$ is $32^\\circ$, which isn’t equal to any angle measure in triangle $ABC$, the triangles can’t be similar.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"408b39ea","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Circles","difficulty":"medium","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"$(x-3)^2+(y+16)^2=289$\nThe graph of the given equation is a circle in the xy-plane. The point $(q,s)$ lies on the circle. Which of the following is NOT a possible value of $q$?","question_text_html":null,"choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$-17$"},{"label":"B","text":"$-3$"},{"label":"C","text":"$3$"},{"label":"D","text":"$17$"}],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Choice A is correct. It’s given that the graph of the equation $(x-3)^2+(y+16)^2=289$ is a circle in the xy-plane. For a circle in the form $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$, its center is $(h,k)$ and its radius is $r$. It follows that $r^2=289$. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields $r=17$. Therefore, the center of this circle is $(3,-16)$ and the radius is $17$. It follows that the x-coordinates of the points that lie on the circle must be within $17$ units of the x-coordinate of the center of the circle. Therefore, the least possible x-coordinate of a point that lies on the circle is $3-17$, or $-14$, and the greatest possible x-coordinate of a point that lies on the circle is $3+17$, or $20$. So for any point $(q,s)$ to lie on the circle, it must be true that $-14\\leq\\le20$. Since $-17$ does not satisfy this inequality, it is NOT a possible value of $q$.\nChoice B is incorrect. Since $-3$ satisfies the inequality $-14\\leq\\le20$, it is a possible value of $q$.\nChoice C is incorrect. Since $3$ satisfies the inequality $-14\\leq\\le20$, it is a possible value of $q$.\nChoice D is incorrect. Since $17$ satisfies the inequality $-14\\leq\\le20$, it is a possible value of $q$.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"124bc42b","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Area and volume","difficulty":"easy","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"Graph of rectangle upper A upper B upper C upper D in the x y plane with the origin labeled O. The x axis ranges from 0 to 10. The y axis ranges from 0 to 10. Refer to long description.\nRectangle $ABCD$ shown has a length of $7$ units and a width of $2$ units. What is the area, in square units, of rectangle $ABCD$?","question_text_html":"<p><figure><svg id=\"uuid-fa2a30e9-528f-49e7-b981-daba735179c9\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"218.005\" height=\"216.36\" viewBox=\"0 0 218.005 216.36\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Graph of rectangle upper 