037q0,.678-.795.678a.8.8,0,0,1-.756-.368q0-.833,1.222-1.657A4.391,4.391,0,0,1,278.272,222.218Zm-1.822,7.2a1.268,1.268,0,0,0,.852.281,1.8,1.8,0,0,0,1.008-.32q.484-.319.485-.649v-2a7.166,7.166,0,0,1-.755.271c-.4.129-.715.243-.941.339a2.024,2.024,0,0,0-.659.485,1.108,1.108,0,0,0-.319.785A1,1,0,0,0,276.45,229.418Z\"></path><path d=\"M283.622,228.42v-8.392a6.207,6.207,0,0,0-.118-1.24q-.1-.309-.988-.31h-.174c-.078,0-.116-.071-.116-.213,0-.207.038-.31.116-.31q.582-.059,1.075-.155a6.052,6.052,0,0,0,.776-.194c.187-.064.346-.126.474-.184a2.948,2.948,0,0,0,.291-.146l.078-.058h.039a.21.21,0,0,1,.155.107.542.542,0,0,1,.1.2,5.334,5.334,0,0,0-.213,1.686v8.838a7.338,7.338,0,0,0,.154,1.724q.039.136.5.262a2.968,2.968,0,0,0,.659.126c.039,0,.065.078.077.232a.821.821,0,0,1-.018.33q-1.94-.1-2.074-.1-.117,0-2.113.1a.467.467,0,0,1-.077-.32c0-.161.025-.242.077-.242a2.853,2.853,0,0,0,.689-.126q.453-.126.494-.262A5.871,5.871,0,0,0,283.622,228.42Z\"></path><path d=\"M291.354,222.218a3.465,3.465,0,0,1,1.569.34,2.626,2.626,0,0,1,1.037.872,3.979,3.979,0,0,1,.524,1.085,3.842,3.842,0,0,1,.164,1.1c0,.259-.038.416-.116.474a.786.786,0,0,1-.446.088H289.2c-.1,0-.156.032-.156.1a3.769,3.769,0,0,0,.737,2.248,2.461,2.461,0,0,0,2.132,1.027,3.737,3.737,0,0,0,.775-.078,3.6,3.6,0,0,0,.63-.184,3.671,3.671,0,0,0,.445-.213,3.784,3.784,0,0,0,.32-.2l.136-.1c.077,0,.116.084.116.252a.7.7,0,0,1-.116.349,3.561,3.561,0,0,1-1.143.978,3.24,3.24,0,0,1-1.648.436,3.683,3.683,0,0,1-2.762-1.211,4.129,4.129,0,0,1-1.153-2.955,4.548,4.548,0,0,1,1.1-3.218A3.582,3.582,0,0,1,291.354,222.218Zm-.194.718a1.706,1.706,0,0,0-1.541.862,2.912,2.912,0,0,0-.533,1.424c0,.091.039.136.117.136h3.779c.064,0,.1-.065.1-.194a2.711,2.711,0,0,0-.524-1.424A1.6,1.6,0,0,0,291.16,222.936Z\"></path><path d=\"M296.615,230.6a1.1,1.1,0,0,1-.339-.8,1.037,1.037,0,0,1,.339-.785,1.131,1.131,0,0,1,.8-.32,1.093,1.093,0,0,1,1.105,1.1,1.133,1.133,0,0,1-.32.8,1.037,1.037,0,0,1-.785.339A1.1,1.1,0,0,1,296.615,230.6Z\"></path></g></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for 2 nonoverlapping triangles with a common vertex\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>Triangle upper L upper M upper R has a common vertex with triangle upper P upper Q upper R.</li><li>The common vertex is upper R.</li><li>A note indicates the figure is not drawn to scale.</li></ul></div></figure></p><p></p><p>In the figure, $\\overline{LQ}$ intersects $\\overline{MP}$ at point $R$, and $\\overline{LM}$ is parallel to $\\overline{PQ}$. The lengths of $\\overline{MR}$ and $\\overline{RP}$ are $8$ and $14$ units, respectively. The area of $△LMR$ is $36$ square units. What is the area of $△PQR$, in square units?</p>","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$\\frac{576}{49}$"},{"label":"B","text":"$\\frac{144}{7}$"},{"label":"C","text":"$63$"},{"label":"D","text":"$\\frac{441}{4}$"}],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Choice D is correct. The figure shows that $∠MRL$ and $∠PRQ$ are vertical angles. Since vertical angles are congruent, $∠MRL$ and $∠PRQ$ are congruent. It’s given that $\\overline{LM}$ is parallel to $\\overline{PQ}$. The figure also shows that $\\overline{LQ}$ intersects $\\overline{LM}$ and $\\overline{PQ}$. If two parallel segments are intersected by a third segment, alternate interior angles are congruent. Thus, $∠MLR$ and $∠PQR$ are congruent. Since $△LMR$ and $△PQR$ have two pairs of congruent angles, the triangles are similar, where $\\overline{MR}$ in $△LMR$ corresponds to $\\overline{PR}$ in $△PQR$. Since the areas of similar triangles are proportional to the squares of the lengths of corresponding sides, it follows that $\\frac{\\text{area}\\text{of}△PQR}{\\text{area}\\text{of}△LMR}=(\\frac{RP}{MR})^2$. It’s given that the lengths of $\\overline{MR}$ and $\\overline{RP}$ are $8$ and $14$ units, respectively, and the area of $△LMR$ is $36$ square units. Substituting $14$ for $RP$, $8$ for $MR$, and $36$ for $\\text{area}\\text{of}△LMR$ in the equation $\\frac{\\text{area}\\text{of}△PQR}{\\text{area}\\text{of}△LMR}=(\\frac{RP}{MR})^2$ yields $\\frac{\\text{area}\\text{of}△PQR}{36}=(\\frac{14}{8})^2$, which is equivalent to $\\frac{\\text{area}\\text{of}△PQR}{36}=(\\frac{7}{4})^2$, or $\\frac{\\text{area}\\text{of}△PQR}{36}=\\frac{49}{16}$. Multiplying each side of this equation by $36$ yields $\\text{area}\\text{of}△PQR=36(\\frac{49}{16})$, or $\\text{area}\\text{of}△PQR=\\frac{441}{4}$. Therefore, the area of $△PQR$ is $\\frac{441}{4}$ square units.\nChoice A is incorrect. This is the result of multiplying $36$ by $(\\frac{8}{14})^2$ instead of by $(\\frac{14}{8})^2$.\nChoice B is incorrect. This is the result of multiplying $36$ by $\\frac{8}{14}$ instead of by $(\\frac{14}{8})^2$.\nChoice C is incorrect. This is the result of multiplying $36$ by $\\frac{14}{8}$ instead of by $(\\frac{14}{8})^2$.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"c8ab85ad","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Area and volume","difficulty":"easy","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"Which expression represents the area, in square inches, of a square with a side length of $35$ inches?","question_text_html":null,"choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$\\sqrt{35}$"},{"label":"B","text":"$35+35$"},{"label":"C","text":"$(4)(35)$"},{"label":"D","text":"$35^2$"}],"correct_answer":"D","explanation":"Choice D is correct. The area of a square is given by $s^2$, where $s$ is the length of each side of the square. It’s given that the length of each side of a square is $35$ inches. Substituting $35$ for $s$ in the expression $s^2$ yields $35^2$. Therefore, the expression that represents the area, in square inches, of the square is $35^2$.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.\nChoice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.\nChoice C is incorrect. This expression represents the perimeter, in inches, of the square rather than the area, in square inches.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"98e02472","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Right triangles and trigonometry","difficulty":"hard","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"In right triangle $XYZ$, angles $X$ and $Z$ are acute angles. Point $R$ lies on $\\overline{XZ}$ such that $\\overline{YR}$ is perpendicular to $\\overline{XZ}$. The measure of $∠XZY$ is $a^\\circ$, the measure of $∠XYR$ is $b^\\circ$, and the length of $\\overline{XY}$ is $19$. If $\\sina^\\circ=\\frac{12}{13}$, what is the value of $\\tanb^\\circ$?","question_text_html":null,"choices":[],"correct_answer":"12/5","explanation":"The correct answer is $\\frac{12}{5}$. It's given that in right triangle $XYZ$, $∠X$ and $∠Z$ are acute angles. It follows that $∠Y$ is a right angle. It's given that point $R$ lies on $XZ$ such that $YR$ is perpendicular to $XZ$. Since $∠Y$ is a right angle and is comprised of $∠XYR$ and $∠ZYR$, it follows that the sum of the measures of $∠XYR$ and $∠ZYR$ is $90^\\circ$. It's given that the measure of $∠XYR$ is $b^\\circ$. It follows that the measure of $∠ZYR$ is $(90-b)^\\circ$. Since $YR$ is perpendicular to $XZ$, it follows that $∠XRY$ and $∠ZRY$ are right angles. It follows that $∆XRY$ and $∆ZRY$ are right triangles. It's given that the measure of $∠XZY$ is $a^\\circ$. Because the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, it follows that the measures of $∠ZRY$, $∠XZY$, and $∠ZYR$ have a sum of $180^\\circ$. Since the measure of $∠ZRY$ is $90^\\circ$, the measure of $∠YZR$ is $a^\\circ$, and the measure of $∠ZYR$ is $(90-b)^\\circ$, it follows that $90+a+90-b=180$, or $a-b+180=180$. Subtracting $180$ from both sides of this equation yields $a-b=0$. Adding $b$ to both sides of this equation yields $a=b$. It's given that $\\sina^\\circ=\\frac{12}{13}$. Since $a=b$, it follows that $\\sinb^\\circ=\\frac{12}{13}$. In a right triangle, the sine of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. It follows that, for a positive constant $k$, $12k$ represents the length of the leg opposite $∠XYR$ and $13k$ represents the length of the hypotenuse of $△XRY$. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. Let $nk$ represent the length of the leg adjacent to $∠XYR$. It follows that $(13k)^2=(12k)^2+(nk)^2$, or $169k^2=144k^2+n^2k^2$. Subtracting $144k^2$ from both sides of this equation yields $25k^2=n^2k^2$. Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields $5k=nk$. Therefore, the length of the leg adjacent to $∠XYR$ is $5k$. In a right triangle, the tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that $\\tanb^\\circ=\\frac{12k}{5k}$, or $\\tanb^\\circ=\\frac{12}{5}$. Note that 12/5 and 2.4 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.","is_grid_in":true},{"source_id":"e6c557f9","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Area and volume","difficulty":"hard","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"The edge length, in inches, of cube Y is $\\frac{3}{88}$ the edge length, in inches, of cube X. The surface area, in square inches, of cube Y is $n$ times the surface area, in square inches, of cube X. What is the value of $n$?","question_text_html":null,"choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$\\frac{9}{7,744}$"},{"label":"B","text":"$\\frac{27}{3,872}$"},{"label":"C","text":"$\\frac{3}{88}$"},{"label":"D","text":"$\\frac{9}{44}$"}],"correct_answer":"A","explanation":"Choice A is correct. Since all cubes are similar to each other, cube X and cube Y are similar figures. In similar figures, if lengths differ by a scale factor of $k$, then areas differ by a scale factor of $k^2$. It’s given that the edge length, in inches, of cube Y is $\\frac{3}{88}$ the edge length, in inches, of cube X. It follows that the surface area, in square inches, of cube Y is $(\\frac{3}{88})^2$, or $\\frac{9}{7,744}$, times the surface area, in square inches, of cube X. Therefore, the value of $n$ is $\\frac{9}{7,744}$.\nAlternate approach: The surface area of a cube is $6s^2$, where $s$ is the edge length. Let $x$ represent the edge length, in inches, of cube X. Substituting $x$ for $s$ in $6s^2$ yields $6x^2$. Therefore, the surface area, in square inches, of cube X can be expressed as $6x^2$. It’s given that the edge length, in inches, of cube Y is $\\frac{3}{88}$ the edge length, in inches, of cube X. Therefore, the edge length, in inches, of cube Y can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{88})x$, or $\\frac{3x}{88}$. Substituting $\\frac{3x}{88}$ for $s$ in $6s^2$ yields $6(\\frac{3x}{88})^2$, which is equivalent to $6(\\frac{9x^2}{7,744})$, or $\\frac{27x^2}{3,872}$. Therefore, the surface area, in square inches, of cube Y can be expressed as $\\frac{27x^2}{3,872}$. It’s given that the surface area, in square inches, of cube Y is $n$ times the surface area, in square inches, of cube X. It follows that $\\frac{27x^2}{3,872}=n(6x^2)$. Dividing both sides of this equation by $6x^2$ yields $\\frac{27x^2}{3,872(6x^2)}=n$, which is equivalent to $\\frac{27x^2}{23,232x^2}=n$, or $\\frac{9}{7,744}=n$. Therefore, the value of $n$ is $\\frac{9}{7,744}$.\nChoice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"4a141e77","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"easy","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"A diagram of 3 lines. Refer to long description.\nIn the figure, line $p$ is parallel to line $r$, and line $t$ intersects both lines. What is the value of $x$?","question_text_html":"<p><figure><svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"236.476364pt\" height=\"254.209542pt\" viewBox=\"0 