9 2822 \nL 1478 2810 \nL 1126 1427 \nz\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"></path></defs><use xlink:href=\"#STIXGeneral-Italic-72\" transform=\"translate(0 0.90625)\"></use></g></g><g id=\"text_6\"><g transform=\"translate(16.711364 16.011562) scale(0.13 -0.13)\"><defs><path id=\"STIXGeneral-Italic-74\" d=\"M 1894 2739 \nL 1862 2534 \nL 1325 2534 \nL 768 435 \nQ 755 384 755 346 \nQ 755 243 851 243 \nQ 928 243 1030 342 \nQ 1133 442 1370 749 \nL 1453 704 \nQ 1158 256 969 93 \nQ 781 -70 538 -70 \nQ 243 -70 243 166 \nQ 243 230 346 640 \nL 845 2534 \nL 365 2534 \nL 358 2573 \nQ 358 2688 570 2733 \nQ 730 2771 992 2979 \nQ 1254 3187 1421 3437 \nQ 1459 3494 1510 3494 \nQ 1568 3494 1568 3443 \nQ 1568 3411 1562 3398 \nL 1382 2739 \nL 1894 2739 \nz\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"></path></defs><use xlink:href=\"#STIXGeneral-Italic-74\" transform=\"translate(0 0.40625)\"></use></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"pe878e157ac\"><rect x=\"7.2\" y=\"13.078804\" width=\"209.25\" height=\"207.9\"></rect></clipPath></defs></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for a diagram of 3 lines\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>Clockwise from top left, the lines are labeled t, p, and r.</li><li>Line t intersects both line p and line r.</li><li>At the intersection of line t and line p, 2 angles are labeled clockwise from top left as follows:<br><ul><li>Top left: x°</li><li>Bottom right: 72°</li></ul></li><li>Note: Figure not drawn to scale.</li></ul></div></figure></p><p>In the figure, line $p$ is parallel to line $r$, and line $t$ intersects both lines. What is the value of $x$?</p>","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$36$"},{"label":"B","text":"$72$"},{"label":"C","text":"$180$"},{"label":"D","text":"$252$"}],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Choice B is correct. In the figure shown, the angles with measures $x^\\circ$ and $72^\\circ$ are vertical angles. Vertical angles have equal measure, so the two marked angles have equal measure. Therefore, the value of $x$ is $72$.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"7ce2e728","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"hard","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"For triangles $ABC$ and $FGH$, angles $B$ and $G$ each measure $51^\\circ$, the length of $\\overline{AB}$ is $6$, and the length of $\\overline{FG}$ is $18$. Which additional piece(s) of information is(are) sufficient to prove that triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $FGH$?\nThe measure of angle $A$ is equal to the measure of angle $F$.\nThe measure of angle $C$ is equal to the measure of angle $H$.\nThe length of $\\overline{FH}$ is $3$ times the length of $\\overline{AC}$.","question_text_html":null,"choices":[{"label":"A","text":"I is sufficient, II is sufficient, and III is sufficient."},{"label":"B","text":"I is sufficient and II is sufficient, but III is not."},{"label":"C","text":"II is sufficient and III is sufficient, but I is not."},{"label":"D","text":"III is sufficient, but I is not and II is not."}],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Choice B is correct. It’s given that in triangles $ABC$ and $FGH$, angles $B$ and $G$ each measure $51^\\circ$, the length of $\\overline{AB}$ is $6$, and the length of $\\overline{FG}$ is $18$. For statement I, if the measure of angle $A$ is equal to the measure of angle $F$, then triangles $ABC$ and $FGH$ have two pairs of congruent angles, angles $A$ and $F$ and angles $B$ and $G$. When $2$ pairs of angles in $2$ triangles are congruent, the triangles are similar, so statement I is sufficient. For statement II, if the measure of angle $C$ is equal to the measure of angle $H$, then triangles $ABC$ and $FGH$ have $2$ pairs of congruent angles, angles $C$ and $H$ and angles $B$ and $G$. When $2$ pairs of angles in $2$ triangles are congruent, the triangles are similar, so statement II is sufficient. For statement III, if the length of $\\overline{FH}$ is $3$ times the length of $\\overline{AC}$, then the ratio of the length of $\\overline{FH}$ to the length of $\\overline{AC}$ is $3$. It’s given that the length of $\\overline{FG}$ is $18$ and the length of $\\overline{AB}$ is $6$. Therefore, the ratio of the length of $\\overline{FG}$ to the length of $\\overline{AB}$ is also $3$, so there are two pairs of sides in proportion. However, angle $B$ isn’t between sides $AB$ and $AC$, and angle $G$ isn’t between sides $FG$ and $FH$. Since the given angle measures aren’t between the proportional sides, this information isn't sufficient to prove that the triangles are similar. Therefore, statements I and II are each sufficient, but statement III isn't sufficient.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"f3f06c3a","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Area and volume","difficulty":"easy","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"A rectangle. Its length is x. Its width is y. Note: Figure not drawn to scale.\nIn the rectangle shown, $x=7$ centimeters and $y=5$ centimeters. What is the area, in square centimeters, of the rectangle?","question_text_html":"<p><figure><svg id=\"uuid-ef640d97-b00e-457b-a1f4-93b9cf1435b1\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"225.612\" height=\"147.135\" viewBox=\"0 0 225.612 147.135\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A rectangle. Its length is x. Its width is y. Note: Figure not drawn to scale.\"><path d=\"M0,143.535v-.63h.195c.23,0,.437-.025.623-.075.185-.05.332-.152.442-.308.11-.155.165-.387.165-.697v-7.35c0-.3-.058-.522-.173-.667-.115-.145-.265-.24-.45-.285-.185-.045-.388-.067-.60