1\"></use><use xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-6c\" x=\"1165.234375\"></use><use xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-65\" x=\"1189.746094\"></use><use xlink:href=\"#CrimsonText-Regular-2e\" x=\"1231.738281\"></use></g></g><g id=\"text_4\"><g transform=\"translate(223.426364 84.154679) scale(0.13 -0.13)\"><defs><path id=\"STIXGeneral-Italic-72\" d=\"M 1126 1427 \nL 1229 1651 \nQ 1446 2131 1830 2534 \nQ 2106 2822 2336 2822 \nQ 2470 2822 2553 2732 \nQ 2637 2643 2637 2496 \nQ 2637 2349 2557 2249 \nQ 2477 2150 2336 2150 \nQ 2214 2150 2112 2317 \nQ 2061 2406 2010 2406 \nQ 1837 2406 1485 1805 \nQ 1267 1434 1136 1104 \nQ 1005 774 774 0 \nL 288 0 \nL 806 1869 \nQ 909 2246 909 2394 \nQ 909 2560 666 2560 \nQ 614 2560 467 2541 \nL 467 2650 \nL 1459 2822 \nL 1478 2810 \nL 1126 1427 \nz\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"></path></defs><use xlink:href=\"#STIXGeneral-Italic-72\" transform=\"translate(0 0.90625)\"></use></g></g><g id=\"text_5\"><g transform=\"translate(223.426364 149.327093) scale(0.13 -0.13)\"><defs><path id=\"STIXGeneral-Italic-73\" d=\"M 2342 2829 \nL 2214 1933 \nL 2112 1933 \nQ 2022 2675 1542 2675 \nQ 1370 2675 1267 2579 \nQ 1165 2483 1165 2310 \nQ 1165 2067 1510 1658 \nQ 1946 1152 1946 787 \nQ 1946 397 1686 163 \nQ 1427 -70 1024 -70 \nQ 838 -70 672 -6 \nQ 538 51 390 51 \nQ 262 51 205 -83 \nL 102 -83 \nL 230 934 \nL 333 934 \nQ 461 64 973 64 \nQ 1203 64 1331 192 \nQ 1459 320 1459 557 \nQ 1459 845 1101 1293 \nQ 698 1798 698 2131 \nQ 698 2451 909 2636 \nQ 1120 2822 1472 2822 \nQ 1600 2822 1830 2758 \nQ 1971 2714 2061 2714 \nQ 2189 2714 2253 2829 \nL 2342 2829 \nz\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"></path></defs><use xlink:href=\"#STIXGeneral-Italic-73\" transform=\"translate(0 0.796875)\"></use></g></g><g id=\"text_6\"><g transform=\"translate(16.711364 16.011562) scale(0.13 -0.13)\"><defs><path id=\"STIXGeneral-Italic-74\" d=\"M 1894 2739 \nL 1862 2534 \nL 1325 2534 \nL 768 435 \nQ 755 384 755 346 \nQ 755 243 851 243 \nQ 928 243 1030 342 \nQ 1133 442 1370 749 \nL 1453 704 \nQ 1158 256 969 93 \nQ 781 -70 538 -70 \nQ 243 -70 243 166 \nQ 243 230 346 640 \nL 845 2534 \nL 365 2534 \nL 358 2573 \nQ 358 2688 570 2733 \nQ 730 2771 992 2979 \nQ 1254 3187 1421 3437 \nQ 1459 3494 1510 3494 \nQ 1568 3494 1568 3443 \nQ 1568 3411 1562 3398 \nL 1382 2739 \nL 1894 2739 \nz\n\" transform=\"scale(0.015625)\"></path></defs><use xlink:href=\"#STIXGeneral-Italic-74\" transform=\"translate(0 0.40625)\"></use></g></g></g></g><defs><clipPath id=\"pa3fb9f1779\"><rect x=\"7.2\" y=\"13.078804\" width=\"209.25\" height=\"207.9\"></rect></clipPath></defs></svg><div role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for diagram of 3 lines\" class=\"sr-only\"><ul><li>Clockwise from top left, the 3 lines are labeled t, r, and s.</li><li>Line t intersects both line r and line s.</li><li>At the intersection of line t and line r, 1 angle is labeled clockwise from top left as follows:<br><ul><li>Bottom right: 24°</li></ul></li><li>At the intersection of line t and line s, 1 angle is labeled clockwise from top left as follows:<br><ul><li>Top left: x°</li></ul></li><li>Note: Figure not drawn to scale.</li></ul></div></figure></p><p>In the figure, line $t$ intersects lines $r$ and $s$. Which additional piece of information is sufficient to prove that lines $r$ and $s$ are parallel?</p>","choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$x<24$"},{"label":"B","text":"$x=24$"},{"label":"C","text":"$x>24$"},{"label":"D","text":"$x-24=360$"}],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Choice B is correct. In the figure shown, transversal $t$ intersects lines $r$ and $s$. Two lines intersected by a transversal are parallel if and only if a pair of alternate interior angles have equal measure. The angle marked $24^\\circ$ and the angle marked $x^\\circ$ form a pair of alternate interior angles. Therefore, $x=24$ is sufficient to prove that lines $r$ and $s$ are parallel.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.\nChoice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.\nChoice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"149021da","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Circles","difficulty":"hard","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"A circle has center $P$, and points $A$ and $B$ lie on the circle. The measure of arc $AB$ is $45^\\circ$ and the length of arc $AB$ is $4\nπ$ units. What is the length, in units, of the radius of the circle?","question_text_html":null,"choices":[],"correct_answer":"16","explanation":"The correct answer is $16$. It’s given that the measure of arc $AB$ is $45^\\circ$ and the length of arc $AB$ is $4π$ units. In a circle, the circumference corresponds to an arc measure of $360^\\circ$. Therefore, the ratio of the measure of arc $AB$ to $360^\\circ$ is equal to the ratio of the length of arc $AB$ to the circumference. Since the circumference of a circle is $2πr$, it follows that $\\frac{45}{360}=\\frac{4π}{2πr}$. This equation can be rewritten as $\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{2}{r}$. Multiplying both sides of this equation by $8r$ yields $1(r)=2(8)$, or $r=16$. Therefore, the length, in units, of the radius of the circle is $16$.","is_grid_in":true},{"source_id":"11301fb6","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Circles","difficulty":"medium","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"The difference between the measure of angle $A$ and the measure of angle $B$ is $-\\frac{5}{6}π$ radians. Which expression shows the difference between the measure of angle $A$ and the measure of angle $B$, in degrees?","question_text_html":null,"choices":[{"label":"A","text":"$-\\frac{5}{6}π\\cdot\\frac{360^\\circ}{π}$"},{"label":"B","text":"$-\\frac{5}{6}π\\cdot\\frac{180^\\circ}{π}$"},{"label":"C","text":"$-\\frac{5}{6}π\\cdot\\frac{π}{180^\\circ}$"},{"label":"D","text":"$-\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot\\frac{180^\\circ}{π}$"}],"correct_answer":"B","explanation":"Choice B is correct. Since $π$ radians $=$ $180^\\circ$, the measure of an angle, in degrees, can be found by multiplying its measure, in radians, by $\\frac{180^\\circ}{π}$. It’s given that the difference between the measure of angle $A$ and the measure of angle $B$ is $-\\frac{5}{6}π$ radians. It follows that the expression $-\\frac{5}{6}\\pi\\cdot\\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}$ shows the difference between the measure of angle $A$ and the measure of angle $B$, in degrees.\nChoice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.\nChoice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.","is_grid_in":false},{"source_id":"697b3954","section":"math","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Lines, angles, and triangles","difficulty":"medium","passage":null,"passage_html":null,"question_text":"Triangle upper A upper B upper C and triangle upper B upper D upper E. Refer to long description.\nIn the figure shown, $△ABC$ is congruent to $△BDE$. If $r=54$ and $s=26$, what is the length of $CD$?","question_text_html":"<p><figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"247.442\" height=\"245.379\" viewBox=\"0 0 247.442 245.379\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Triangle upper A upper B upper C and triangle upper B upper D upper E. Refer to long description.\"><g id=\"a70fa6a5-dab9-4b14-8d2e-81ccb4d5e22d\"><polygon points=\"131.141 182.84 32.562 182.84 32.562 17.587 131.141 182.84\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#231f20\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"1.89\"></polygon><polyline points=\"32.562 182.548 220.239 100.213 220.239 182.84 130.905 182.84\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#231f20\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"1.89\"></polyline><path d=\"M10.291,230.823a7.571,7.571,0,0,0-.136-1.705c-.039-.1-.261-.2-.669-.3a4.413,4.413,0,0,0-.862-.145c-.052,0-.078-.074-.078-.223a.429.429,0,0,1,.078-.3q.252.019,1.008.068t1.279.049c.336,0,.758-.02,1.269-.059s.811-.058.9-.058a.957.957,0,0,1,.038.29c0,.156-.026.233-.078.233a4.866,4.866,0,